Antibiotics is a widely used drugs in treatment of bacterial infections. But in recent years, the abuse of antibiotics has led to many biology and medicine problems, and superbacteria is one of the most typical case. $^{1-4}$ Nitrofurazone (NZF) is a synthesized antibiotic with a broad-spectrum antibiotic, which is commonly used in animal husbandry and aquaculture. Recent research shows that long-term ingestion of NZF can cause many kinds of diseases, such as cancer, tumour and other side effects. $5^{-7}$ As a result, it had been banned in USA, China, European Union and many other countries. However, NZF was still found in aquatic product and groundwater, and the residual NZF could be transmitted to human bodies through the food chain. Hence, detection of residual NZF in aquatic products and domestic water is important. Up to now, the detection of NZF is mainly based on instrumental methods, such as capillary electrophoresis, Raman spectroscopy, ion mobility spectrometry and liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry.8−10 These methods are expensive and inconvenient. Therefore, it is extremely urgent to find an effective method for NZF detection, of which MOFs were considered as good candidates to efficiently detect NZF.11
Actually, luminescent metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are of bright promise as new luminescent probes because of their high sensitivity, high selectivity and reusability.12−17 These kaleidoscopic luminescent MOFs had applied in detection of metal ions, explosives, small molecules, temperature, cancer marker and so on.18−26 Among these studies, interpenetrated MOFs have been widely explored.27−31 However, through interpenetrationcontrol to switch luminescent properties was seldom studied. Furthermore, controlling the degree of MOFs interpenetration to detect pollution in water via an environmentally friendly manner is a challenge, and never reported.32−35
In this contribution, a novel three-dimensional (3D) anionic frameworks $\mathrm{\{(Me_{2}N H_{2})[I n(B C P)]{\cdot2D M F}_{\mathrm{{Jn}}}}}$ (V101) was obtained from a solvothermal reaction between organic ligand 5-(2,6-bis(4-carboxyphenyl) pyridin- $^{\cdot}4$ -yl) isophthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{4}B C P)}$ and $\mathrm{{In}(N O_{3})_{3}}$ in the DMF solvent at $\mathrm{{120~^{\circ}C}}$ . With rationally tuned the reaction solvent from DMF to DEF, a new robust noninterpenetration framework $\mathrm{\{(Me_{2}N H_{2})[I n(B C P)]{\cdot}2.5D E F_{\mathrm{{Jn}}}}}$ (V102) was constructed. Both compounds V101 and V102 exhibit high thermal stability and solvent stability. Interestingly, luminescent explorations revealed that V102 represented excellent luminescent properties, which can sensitivity and selectivity detect NZF in water solution. In contrast, interpenetrated framework V101 cannot detect NZF in water. This is the first example of interpenetration-depended luminescent probe in MOFs to detect NZF.
Single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis indicates that V101 crystallizes in the triclinic system with space group P-1. The asymmetric unit contains one tetradentate ligand $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{BCP}$ and one crystallographically independent $\mathrm{In^{3^{+}}}$ ion, and the oxidation state of In ion was confirmed with typical bonding energy of $452.8$ ev $\mathrm{(3d_{3/2})}$ by XPS spectra (Figure. S3). Each $\scriptstyle\mathbf{In3^{+}}$ ion is coordinated to eight oxygen atoms of four carboxylate groups from four BCP4- , and each $\mathrm{BCP4^{-}}$ connects with four separate $\mathrm{In^{3^{+}}}$ ions (Figure. 1a1), forming a 3D two-fold interpenetration framework with $^{(4,4)}$ -connected sqc-type topology (Figure. $\mathrm{\mathbf{1}}\mathrm{b_{1}}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ). The eight In-O bond distances range from $2.187$ to 2.400 $\mathrm{\AA}$ , which are in good agreement with those reported previously.36,37 The anionic framework is balanced by $\mathrm{[(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}]^{+}}$ counterions, which generated from in situ decomposition of DMF.38,39 V101 contains a large one-dimensional (1D) channel along the $a$ axes with approximate diameter of $6.8\mathrm{\AA}$ (Figure. S1). Due to the twofold interpenetration, the available sizes of channels will be reduced. Even so, the total free volume of V101 is estimated to be $42.1\%$ as determined by PLATON routine after removal of the guest solvent molecules.40,41 Noninterpenetrated structure V102 crystallizes in the orthorhombic system with space group Fddd. Similar with V101, each $\mathrm{In^{3^{+}}}$ ion is coordinated to eight oxygen atoms of four carboxylate groups, and each $\mathrm{BCP4^{-}}$ connected with four separate $\mathrm{In^{3^{+}}}$ ions (Figure. 1a2), forming a 3D non-interpenetration framework. The $\left[\mathrm{In(O_{2}C)_{4}}\right]$ unit can be regarded as a tetrahedral four-connected node with In-O distances ranging from 2.073 to 2.495 Å. The anionic framework was balanced by $\mathrm{[(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}]^{+}}$ counterions too. Along $a$ direction, a large 1D open channel possesses an approximate diameter of $9.5\textup{\AA}$ (Figure. S2), and interestingly along $c$ direction, the channel with a diameter of $9.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ also is observed. Benefitting from the non-interpenetrated structure, the total solventaccessible volume of V102 is approximately $67.2\%$ , which calculated by the PLATON software after removing all of the guest molecules from the channels. $^{40,41}$
Different angels between four carboxylate groups and $\mathrm{In^{3^{+}}}$ ion can be observed in compounds V101 and V102, which are marked as $\mathrm{C_{1}I n C_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{1}I n C_{3}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{1}I n C_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}I n C_{3}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}I n C_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}I n C_{4}}$ (Figure. $\bf{1}a_{1}$ and $\mathbf{a}_{2}$ ).  For compound V101, these angels are $92.7^{\circ}$ , $106.6^{\circ}$ , $\boldsymbol{137.4^{\circ}}$ , $136.8^{\circ}$ , $\mathbf{102.0^{0}}$ and $89.5^{\circ}$ , respectively. While for compound V102, these angels are $95.0^{\circ}$ , $\bf{106.1^{o}}$ , $\boldsymbol{133.4^{\circ}}$ , $\boldsymbol{133.4^{\circ}}$ , $\bf{100.0^{0}}$ and $95.0^{\circ}$ , respectively. These divergences maybe lead to the different growing direction, and ultimately get different crystal structures.
PXRD measurements of V101 and V102 were recorded in Figure. S4. The patterns are in conformity with the simulated ones, indicating that as-synthesized V101 and V102 are pure phases. In order to investigate the stabilities of interpenetrated framework V101 and noninterpenetrated framework V102 in solvent, these samples were immersed in DMA, DMF, acetone, methanol, dioxane, dichloromethane, cyclohexane, 1- methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP), water, ethanol and ethyl acetate at room temperature for 10 h. PXRD patterns of V101 and V102 are still consistent with the simulated ones, suggesting that V101 and V102 have excellent solvent stability (Figure. S7 and Figure. S8). Furthermore, to explore the acid/base stability of V101 and V102, they were immersed in different acid or basic solution. The experimental PXRD patterns are in good agreement with the simulated ones, indicating that V101 remains stable between pH 2-12 (Figure. S7), and V102 can keep stable in spanning the $\mathrm{pH}$ range 4-9 (Figure. S8). Their thermal stabilities were also studied. PXRD patterns of V101 and V102 showed that the crystalline structures still reamined intact even kept $3\mathrm{~h~}$ at $473\ \mathrm{K},$ which can also be confirmed by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). The TGA curve of V101 showed that a weight loss of $18.2\%$ occurred in the temperature range from 40 to ${}^{220}{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is corresponding to the loss of two DMF molecules in the channel (calculated $18.6\%)$ . Also, from 40 to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , a weight loss of $27.6\%$ can be observed in the TGA curves of V102, which is attributed to the removal of two DEF molecules in the channel (calculated $28.3\%$ . The frameworks of V101 and V102 still keep stable at 400 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure. S5), revealing that these anionic frameworks have a good thermal stability.
In addition, to explore the detection limit of V102 as a NZF probe, a series of concentrations of NZF solution were prepared (1-500 ppm) in distilled water. The luminescence intensity of V102 gradually decreased with increasing the concentration of NZF. The decreased luminescence intensity still could be clearly observed when V102 was immersed in a 0.2 ppm NZF solution (Figure. 3a). This indicated that compound V102 have a good application prospect in detected NZF in water.
Recyclability of luminescent probes is an important requirement in practical applications.42 Herein, the recycling performance of compound V102 as a NZF luminescent probe was investigated. After detection of NZF,
V102 was collected by centrifugation and washed with alcohol for several times. Then, a new detection procedure could be performed. As shown in Figure. 3b, NZF can be detected even after five recycles. Meanwhile, PXRD patterns were well conformity with the simulated ones, indicating that the framework V102 still remain stable during recycle experiments (Figure. S16). On the other hand, the luminescent intensity of V102 is almost unchanged with time increasing in water (Figure S12). Furthermore, inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometer was applied during the analysis of $\mathrm{In^{3^{+}}}$ leaching, and $\mathrm{In^{3+}}$ ion was not detected in the solution after centrifugation. These confirming the high stability and recyclability of V102 as a luminescent probe in water.
Then, the possible quenching mechanism was proposed. Firstly, PXRD patterns showed that V102 still keep crystal state after luminescent detection of NZF, proved that the luminescence quenching was not caused by framework collapse.43 Secondly, the collision interaction between structures and NZF consume the energy and reduce the luminescent intensity.11,44,45 To further explore the quenching effect of NZF on the emission, the relationship between luminescence quenching efficiency and NZF concentration was quantitatively explained by the Stern-Volmer equation. $46{-}48$ The SternVolmer equation deviated from linearity and bend upward at higher concentrations (Figure. S17), these phenomena indicating that the coexistence of the dynamic and static quenching processes. $49-51$
In conclusion, with changing the reaction solvent from DMF to DEF, two-fold interpenetrated MOF V101 was successfully tuned to non-interpenetrated MOF V102, and these materials showed excellent thermal stability and solvent stability. Surprisingly, compound V102 can selectively detect traces of NZF in water at room temperature, and the detection limit can reach to 0.2 ppm. In contrast, compound V101 cannot detect NZF. More importantly, V102 still kept stable crystalline even after five recycles. This is the first example for interpenetration-depended luminescent probe in MOFs to detect NZF. This work provides a new facile method to tune MOFs for detection of antibiotics in water, which has potentially applied in monitoring water quality.
